General elections were held in [[Argentina]] on Sunday, 23 October 2011. Incumbent president [[Cristina Fernández de Kirchner]] of the [[Front for Victory]] won in a [[Landslide victory|landslide]], with 54% of the vote, securing a second term in office. The [[Front for Victory]] won just over half of the seats in the [[Argentine National Congress|National Congress]].

[[Mercosur Parliament]]arians were also popularly elected for the first time. Another novelty was the introduction of open, simultaneous and mandatory primaries. These took place 14 August 2011 to select the candidates of each political [[List of political parties in Argentina|party or coalition]].<ref name="ReformaPolítica">{{in lang|es}} [http://www.mininterior.gov.ar/asuntos_politicos_y_alectorales/dine/infogral/archivos_legislacion/Ley_26571_.pdf Ley de Democratización de la Representación Política, la Transparencia y la Equidad Electoral]</ref>

==Presidential campaign==
[[File:Boletas para las elecciones primarias en Argentina de 2011.jpg|thumb|Ballots used in the primary elections on 14 August.]]
The nation's myriad parties forged seven coalitions, of which five became contenders for a possible runoff election:
*'''[[Front for Victory]]''': the ruling party, led by President [[Cristina Fernández de Kirchner]], and allies, including the [[New Encounter]].<ref>{{cite web|url=http://m24digital.com/2011/06/14/sabbatella-y-el-fpv-tendran-una-misma-lista-de-diputados-en-la-provincia/ |title=Sabbatella y el FPV tendrán una misma lista de diputados en la Provincia |work=M24 Digital |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110618140837/http://m24digital.com/2011/06/14/sabbatella-y-el-fpv-tendran-una-misma-lista-de-diputados-en-la-provincia/ |archive-date=18 June 2011 }}</ref> The FPV is mostly based on the center-left [[Justicialist Party]] (PJ) factions that support the current government.
*'''[[Federal Peronism]]''', or ''Dissident Peronism'': centrist or conservative PJ figures opposed to the government and allies, including the [[Republican Proposal]]. This coalition remained divided between [[Eduardo Duhalde]]'s '''[[Unión Popular|Popular Front]]''' and [[Alberto Rodríguez Saá]]'s '''Federal Commitment''' both before and after the August primaries.
*'''Union for Social Development''': the [[Radical Civic Union]] (UCR), led by Congressman [[Ricardo Alfonsín]], and allies, which initially included Federal Peronist [[Francisco de Narváez]].
*'''[[Broad Progressive Front (Argentina)|Broad Progressive Front]]''': the [[Socialist Party (Argentina)|Socialist Party]], led by Governor [[Hermes Binner]], and allies, including [[Generation for a National Encounter|GEN]] and the [[Luis Juez|New Party]]. [[Proyecto Sur]] had briefly joined this coalition.
*'''[[Civic Coalition (Argentina)|Civic Coalition]]''': the party, led by Congresswoman [[Elisa Carrió]], had been part of the [[Civic and Social Agreement]], but separated from the latter in August 2010.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.lanacion.com.ar/nota.asp?nota_id=1293981|title=Con más críticas, Carrió se aleja del Acuerdo Cívico|work=La Nación|access-date=30 May 2011|archive-date=23 December 2014|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20141223070312/http://www.lanacion.com.ar/1293981-con-mas-criticas-carrio-se-aleja-del-acuerdo-civico|url-status=dead}}</ref>

Other coalitions of note include the '''[[Workers' Left Front]]''', led by [[Jorge Altamira]], and '''[[Proyecto Sur]]''', led by [[Pino Solanas]]; the latter left the Socialist Party-led coalition and instead formed an alliance with the [[Workers' Socialist Movement (Argentina)|MST]] and the [[Authentic Socialist Party (Argentina)|PSA]].<ref name=m24b>{{cite web|url=http://m24digital.com/2011/06/16/movimiento-proyecto-sur-se-fracciono-entre-binner-y-pino-solanas/ |title=Movimiento Proyecto Sur se fraccionó entre Binner y Pino Solanas |work=M24 Digital |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110711213549/http://m24digital.com/2011/06/16/movimiento-proyecto-sur-se-fracciono-entre-binner-y-pino-solanas/ |archive-date=11 July 2011 }}</ref>

The '''[[Civic and Social Agreement]]''' was an alliance between the UCR and most of what became the Progressive Ample Front and the Civic Coalition, with other, minor allies. This coalition proved unwieldy as the 2011 campaign progressed, however, though various forms of it will be retained in certain provinces for strategic purposes.<ref name=herald1>{{cite web|url=http://www.buenosairesherald.com/article/68956/i-dont-feel-guilty-says-alfons%C3%ADn-after-ucrsocialist-breakup|title=Alfonsín picks economist Javier González Fraga as running mate|work=Buenos Aires Herald}}</ref>

===Front for Victory (incumbents)===
The Front for Victory (FPV) candidate for the [[Justicialist Party]] primaries was current President [[Cristina Fernández de Kirchner]]. Her husband and predecessor, [[Néstor Kirchner]], was considered a top candidate to succeed her until his [[Death and state funeral of Néstor Kirchner|death on 27 October 2010]].<ref>{{cite web|url=http://english.aljazeera.net/news/americas/2010/10/2010102713264244362.html|title=Argentine ex-leader Kirchner dies|work=Al Jazeera News}}</ref>

She had suffered a significant decline in approval during the [[2008 Argentine government conflict with the agricultural sector]] and the subsequent recession, and the ruling [[Front for Victory]] lost its absolute majority in both houses of Congress during the [[2009 Argentine legislative election|June 2009 mid-term elections]].<ref>{{cite news|url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/2/hi/americas/8123057.stm|title=Argentine head set for poll blow|work=BBC News | date=29 June 2009}}</ref> The economy, and her approval ratings, recovered steadily during 2010,<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.urgente24.com/noticias/val/7823/poliarquia-cristina-comenzo-a-bajar-en-las-encuestas.html|title=Poliarquía: Cristina comenzó a bajar en las encuestas|work=Urgente24|access-date=4 June 2011|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110505232432/http://www.urgente24.com/noticias/val/7823/poliarquia-cristina-comenzo-a-bajar-en-las-encuestas.html|archive-date=5 May 2011|url-status=dead}}</ref> however, and the 2011 electoral season began with Fernández de Kirchner's job approval at around 58 percent,<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.google.com/hostednews/epa/article/ALeqM5ghQ_2TZhTxJXDzIWu_8ckl4f_37g?docId=1536978|archive-url=https://archive.today/20130125140203/http://www.google.com/hostednews/epa/article/ALeqM5ghQ_2TZhTxJXDzIWu_8ckl4f_37g?docId=1536978|url-status=dead|archive-date=25 January 2013|title=La imagen positiva de Fernández sube a niveles de comienzos de su Gobierno|publisher=Agencia EFE}}</ref> with polling indicating that she would likely be reelected in the first round.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.elargentino.com/Content.aspx?Id=139201 |title=Cristina, en todas las encuestas, gana cómoda en primera vuelta |work=Diagonales |url-status=usurped |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160304122534/http://www.elargentino.com/Content.aspx?Id=139201 |archive-date=4 March 2016 }}</ref>

She avoided committing herself to running for a second term during the early months of 2011.<ref name=economist>{{cite news|url=https://www.economist.com/the-americas/2011/05/26/will-she-wont-she|title=Will she, won't she?|newspaper=The Economist | date=26 May 2011}}</ref> Two days before the 23 June deadline, however, she announced her decision to run for reelection.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.buenosairesherald.com/article/70760/cfk-announces-she-will-be-seeking-reelection|title=CFK announces she will be seeking reelection|work=Buenos Aires Herald}}</ref> She nominated the nation's [[Minister of Economy of Argentina|Economy Minister]], [[Amado Boudou]], as her running mate on 25 June.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.clarin.com/politica/elecciones/elecciones_2011-candidato_a_vicepresidente-acto_Olivos-companero_formula_Cristina_0_505749697.html|title=Cristina eligió a Boudou como su compañero de fórmula|work=Clarín|date=25 June 2011 }}</ref> Their ticket won a landslide victory in the 14 August primaries, obtaining just over 50% and besting the runner-up (Alfonsín) by nearly 38%; they won in the City of Buenos Aires and in every province except [[San Luis Province|San Luis]] (won by Rodríguez Saá).<ref name=cnn1>{{cite news|url=http://articles.cnn.com/2011-08-15/world/argentina.primary_1_primary-vote-president-cristina-fernandez-peronist?_s=PM:WORLD|title=Fernández wins Argentina primary, looks poised for re-election|publisher=CNN|access-date=21 June 2012|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110916214854/http://articles.cnn.com/2011-08-15/world/argentina.primary_1_primary-vote-president-cristina-fernandez-peronist?_s=PM:WORLD|archive-date=16 September 2011|url-status=dead}}</ref>

Support for Fernández de Kirchner was strongest among the poor (65.2%) and those aged 30 to 44 (54.6%). Her support was weakest among the upper middle class (43.5%), though she remained over 24% ahead of the runner-up (Binner) among those polled within that segment.<ref name=laborda>{{cite news|url=http://www.lanacion.com.ar/1415547-como-sera-el-voto-a-la-oposicion|title=Cómo será el voto a la oposición|work=La Nación}}</ref>

===Federal Peronists===
The leaders of the center-right [[Federal Peronism]] were torn between running for primary elections within the PJ against the Front for Victory, or running instead in the general election through another political alliance. Former President [[Eduardo Duhalde]] was the first to informally start his pre-candidacy campaign, announcing hypothetical cabinet picks as early as December 2009.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://tn.com.ar/politica/00027483/eduardo-duhalde-anuncio-a-su-posible-gabinete|title=Eduardo Duhalde anunció a su posible Gabinete|work=TN Noticias|access-date=22 June 2011|archive-date=9 May 2013|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130509174826/http://tn.com.ar/politica/eduardo-duhalde-anuncio-a-su-posible-gabinete_027483|url-status=dead}}</ref> The Governors of [[Chubut Province|Chubut]], [[Mario Das Neves]], and of [[San Luis Province|San Luis]], [[Alberto Rodríguez Saá]], as well as former Governor of Buenos Aires Province [[Felipe Solá]], also stated their intention to run for president. Das Neves became the first Federal Peronist to drop out, while Solá boosted his own prospects by securing an alliance with the conservative [[Republican Proposal]] (PRO) on 16 May.<ref name=razon>{{cite web|url=http://www.larazon.com.ar/economia/PRO-iria-Sola-candidato_0_240900017.html|title=El PRO iría con Solá de candidato|work=La Razón|access-date=30 May 2011|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110927214635/http://www.larazon.com.ar/economia/PRO-iria-Sola-candidato_0_240900017.html|archive-date=27 September 2011|url-status=dead}}</ref> Duhalde narrowly defeated Rodríguez Saá in a Buenos Aires Federal Peronism primary held on 22 May, though both men remained front-runners for their party's nomination.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.buenosairesherald.com/article/63425/higher-turnout-than-expected-in-federal-peronism-regional-primaries|title=Duhalde beats Rodríguez Saá in dissident PJ primaries by narrow margin|work=Buenos Aires Herald}}</ref> Ultimately, each ran on separate Federal Peronist tickets.

Duhalde formally announced his [[Unión Popular|Popular Union]] candidacy on 9 June, nominating Das Neves as his running mate.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.buenosairesherald.com/article/69617/eduardo-duhalde-officially-launches-presidential-campaign|title=Eduardo Duhalde officially launches presidential campaign|work=Buenos Aires Herald}}</ref> Rodríguez Saá, in turn, nominated former [[List of Governors of Santa Fe|Santa Fe Governor]] [[José María Vernet]] as his running mate on his Federal Commitment ticket.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.buenosairesherald.com/article/71078/vernet-confirms-he-will-be-rodr%C3%ADguez-sa%C3%A1%E2%80%99s-running-mate|title=Vernet confirms he will be Rodríguez Saá's running mate|work=Buenos Aires Herald}}</ref> Solá, who struggled in the polls, withdrew on 11 June, encouraging local candidates in his fold to form alliances with Duhalde and the party's candidate for Buenos Aires Governor, [[Francisco de Narváez]].<ref>{{cite news|url=http://www.lanacion.com.ar/1380708-felipe-sola-descarto-pelear-por-la-casa-rosada|title=Felipe Solá descartó pelear por la Casa Rosada|work=La Nación}}</ref> De Narváez later endorsed Rodríguez Saá.<ref name=juntos>{{cite web|url=http://www.elargentino.com/nota-161323-Rodriguez-Saa-y-De-Narvaez-juntos-y-de-campana.html |title=Rodríguez Saá y De Narváez, juntos y de campaña |work=El Argentino |url-status=usurped |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160304122945/http://www.elargentino.com/nota-161323-Rodriguez-Saa-y-De-Narvaez-juntos-y-de-campana.html |archive-date=4 March 2016 }}</ref>

Support for Duhalde was strongest among the working class (14.2%) and weakest among young voters (3.9%).<ref name=laborda/> Rodríguez Saá polled best among upper middle class voters (14%) and those age 30 to 44 (11.9%); worst among the poor.<ref name=laborda/>

===Radical Civic Union===
The center-left [[Radical Civic Union]] had scheduled primaries for 28 April. Both [[Ricardo Alfonsín]], son of the late former President [[Raúl Alfonsín]], and current party leader [[Ernesto Sanz]] started pre-candidacy campaigns; Sanz, however, dropped out on 28 April. [[Vice President of Argentina|Vice President]] [[Julio Cobos]], considered a likely UCR primary candidate, had stated his intention to run only in August, during the coalition primaries; he dropped out in April as well.<ref name=economist/>

The UCR and the [[Socialist Party (Argentina)|Socialist Party]] (partners in the Civic and Social Agreement) parted ways in May 2011, with Alfonsín and [[Santa Fe Province|Santa Fe]] Governor [[Hermes Binner]] running on separate slates for the primaries in August, and likely in the general election, as well.<ref name=herald1/> Alfonsín secured an alliance with Federal Peronist candidate Francisco de Narváez in [[Buenos Aires Province]],<ref name=recado>{{cite web|url=http://elrecado.net/politica/5020-de-narvaez-seria-el-gobernador-de-alfonsin|title=de Narváez sería el gobernador de Alfonsín|work=El Reacado|access-date=4 June 2011|archive-date=2 April 2012|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120402011506/http://elrecado.net/politica/5020-de-narvaez-seria-el-gobernador-de-alfonsin|url-status=usurped}}</ref> De Narváez ran for governor with his senior partner's endorsement in return for his support for Alfonsín's presidential campaign.<ref name=recado/><ref name=ambito/> Alfonsín nominated former [[Central Bank of Argentina|Central Bank]] President [[Javier González Fraga]], a non-partisan economist close to both the UCR and Federal Peronism, as his running-mate on 2 June.<ref name=herald1/>

De Narváez withdrew his endorsement of Alfonsín in favor of Rodríguez Saá following the 14 August primaries,<ref name=juntos/> though he continued his campaign for Governor of Buenos Aires with Alfonsín's endorsement.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.diariolavozdezarate.com/2011/10/optimismo-de-udeso-de-cara-a-las-elecciones-de-octubre/|title=Optimismo de UDESO de cara a las elecciones de octubre|work=La Voz}}</ref>

Alfonsín's support was strongest among those age 45 to 59 (14.6%), and weakest among young voters (5.3%).<ref name=laborda/>

===Socialists===
Binner endorsed GEN leader [[Margarita Stolbizer]] for Governor of Buenos Aires following his break with Alfonsín,<ref name=ambito>{{cite web|url=http://www.ambito.com/diario/noticia.asp?id=585825|title=Frente progresista ya ensaya fórmula Binner-Stolbizer|work=Ámbito Financiero|access-date=4 June 2011|archive-date=18 March 2012|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120318235347/http://www.ambito.com/diario/noticia.asp?id=585825|url-status=dead}}</ref> and formally announced his [[Broad Progressive Front (Argentina)|Broad Progressive Front]] candidacy on 11 June; he nominated [[Córdoba Province (Argentina)|Córdoba]] Senator [[Norma Morandini]] as his running mate.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.buenosairesherald.com/article/69771/binner-launches-candidacy-for-president|title=Binner officializes presidential candidacy, Morandini running mate|work=Buenos Aires Herald}}</ref> His alliance with [[Pino Solanas]] was dissolved the following week, however, and the [[Proyecto Sur]] leader instead joined a coalition of minor, left-wing parties.<ref name=m24b/>

Binner, despite obtaining fourth place, fared better than expected by local analysts in the 14 August primary,<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.milenio.com/cdb/doc/noticias2011/35827121af07a8b13c03cbc2bbb05745|title=Sondeos pronostican amplio triunfo de Cristina Fernández|work=Milenio}}</ref> and became the runner-up in subsequent polls. His support was strongest among the middle (18.8%) and upper middle classes (18.9%), while weakest among the poor (6.5%); among the broad age groups, voters 30 to 44 were the most supportive (19.3%).<ref name=laborda/>

===Civic Coalition===
The leader of the centrist [[Civic Coalition (Argentina)|Civic Coalition]], [[Elisa Carrió]], reversed her earlier intention to opt out of the 2011 race, and following the departure of her Civic Coalition from the [[Civic and Social Agreement]] formed in 2009 with the UCR, she announced her candidacy for president on 12 December 2010.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.elisacarrio.com.ar/index.asp?seccion=visor_noticia&id=320&descripcion=Sala |title=Se lanzó la candidatura de Carrió-Pérez |publisher=Coalición Cívica |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110629194658/http://www.elisacarrio.com.ar/index.asp?seccion=visor_noticia&id=320&descripcion=Sala |archive-date=29 June 2011 }}</ref> Carrió withdrew her presidential bid following a poor showing in the 14 August primaries, where she obtained 3%.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.buenosairesherald.com/article/76040/after-18-years-i-no-longer-have-power-carri%C3%B3-says|title='After 18 years, I no longer have power,' Carrió says|work=Buenos Aires Herald}}</ref>

===Other candidates===
Numerous other candidates, or potential candidates, dropped out in May 2011, notably Buenos Aires Mayor [[Mauricio Macri]], who instead sought a second term as mayor, and left-wing film maker [[Fernando Solanas]] (who ran unsuccessfully for the same post).<ref name=m24a/> Solanas nominated Congresswoman [[Alcira Argumedo]] as [[Proyecto Sur]]'s candidate for president on 22 June.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.buenosairesherald.com/article/70871/proyecto-sur-breaks-off-with-progressive-front|title=Proyecto Sur breaks off with Progressive Front|work=Buenos Aires Herald}}</ref> The 14 August primary effectively ended Argumedo's campaign, as well as those of Neighbors' Action Movement (MAV) candidate Sergio Pastore, and People's Countryside Party (PCP) candidate José Bonacci; neither had reached the requisite 1.5% threshold needed to advance to the general election.<ref name=baherald/>

The candidate for the [[Workers' Left Front]] (FIT), [[Jorge Altamira]], fared unexpectedly well and advanced to the general election.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.buenosairesherald.com/article/75992/altamira-weve-reached-our-aim-dedicates-it-to-mariano-ferreyra|title=Altamira: 'We've reached our aim', dedicates it to Mariano Ferreyra|work=Buenos Aires Herald}}</ref> Altamira polled best among the poor (7.9%) and among the upper middle class (5.4%).<ref name=laborda/>

