=== Senate ===
Eight districts ([[Buenos Aires Province]], [[Formosa Province|Formosa]], [[Jujuy Province|Jujuy]], [[La Rioja Province (Argentina)|La Rioja]], [[Misiones Province|Misiones]], [[San Juan Province (Argentina)|San Juan]], [[San Luis Province|San Luis]] and [[Santa Cruz Province (Argentina)|Santa Cruz]]) also elected three [[Argentine Senate|National Senators]] each (two for the most voted party or coalition, one for the second most voted party or coalition), to renew a third of the upper house.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.towsa.com/andy/totalpais/2011.html|title=Elecciones en Argentina 2011: Cargos a renovar|publisher=Atlas Electoral de Andy Tow|access-date=22 June 2011|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120513144913/http://towsa.com/andy/totalpais/2011.html|archive-date=13 May 2012|url-status=dead}}</ref>

The opposition fared better in the Senate, which remained nearly unchanged; the upper house would continue divided between the FpV with a majority of 40 seats (out of 72), and the UCR (around 16) and others with the remainder.<ref name=congress/><ref name=tiempo/> The departure of Vice President [[Julio Cobos]] of the UCR (distanced politically from the President since 2008) deprived the opposition of a tie-breaking vote in the Senate.<ref name=tiempo/>

